A multi-module repository (two independent modules, NO go.work): the root
module example.com/foo and a child module example.com/bar under sub/. The
ONLY vulnerability is a Go standard-library issue in the ROOT module's
net/http usage: GO-2024-2687 / CVE-2023-45288 (HTTP/2 CONTINUATION flood),
fixed in go1.21.9. The child module is benign. The desired remediation is
root go.mod `go 1.21.0` -> `go 1.21.9`.

EXPECTED: FAIL.

GAP: the vulncheck bot script cannot remediate standard-library vulnerabilities,
and being in a multi-module repo does not change that. It only knows how to
`go get` module versions, but stdlib fixes come from the Go toolchain/go
directive. With GOTOOLCHAIN=local and an installed go1.26, the scan is evaluated
against the running toolchain, so it never even reports the go1.21.9 fix (that
`Fixed in:` line reads `go1.21.9` with no `@`, so the awk would skip it anyway);
instead it reports newer stdlib CVEs whose `Fixed in:` lines look like
`crypto/tls@go1.26.5`. The awk matches those (they contain `@`) and the script
runs `go get crypto/tls@go1.26.5`, which errors ("can't request explicit version
of standard library package"), short-circuits the `&& go mod tidy`, and leaves
the tree unchanged. The script therefore produces an EMPTY diff while the
want_diff bumps the root go directive, so the case fails.
-- go.mod --
module example.com/foo

go 1.21.0
-- main.go --
package main

import "net/http"

// net/http is the vulnerable stdlib package (GO-2024-2687); the only fix is a
// go-directive bump, which the bot cannot perform.
func main() {
	http.Get("http://example.com/")
}
-- sub/go.mod --
module example.com/bar

go 1.22.0
-- sub/bar.go --
package bar

import "strings"

// Greet returns a friendly greeting for name.
func Greet(name string) string {
	return strings.TrimSpace("hello " + name)
}
-- want_diff.txt --
diff --git a/go.mod b/go.mod
index 9b981c3..d7e9510 100644
--- a/go.mod
+++ b/go.mod
@@ -1,3 +1,3 @@
 module example.com/foo

-go 1.21.0
+go 1.21.9
